<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mustafa Reşid Pasha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1800">1800</a>–<a href="/years/1858">1858</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mustafa Reshid Pasha, Reşid Pasha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mustafa Reşid Pasha was an Ottoman statesman and diplomat, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, known best as the chief architect of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanzimat">Tanzimat</a> reforms. He served as ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the United Kingdom, became minister for foreign affairs in 1837 and was one of the main authors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-gulhane">Edict of Gülhane</a> of 1839, which opened the Tanzimat era. Between 1845 and 1857 he held the office of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a> six times, setting up commercial courts and codes on French models, a committee of education meant to reduce the influence of the ulema, and an Academy of Sciences in 1852. He died of a heart attack in 1858, and his protégés Fuad Pasha and Mehmed Emin Âli Pasha carried the reforms on.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mustafa-resid-pasha.jpg" alt="Mustafa Reşid Pasha" width="330" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Resid_pasha_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mustafa Reşid was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 13 March 1800. His father, a civil servant, died when he was ten, and he left his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> to live with his uncle Ispartalı Ali Pasha, a court chamberlain under Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii">Mahmud II</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a>. He studied at a scribal institution and went with his uncle when the latter became governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> in 1816. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-war-of-independence">Greek War of Independence</a> he noted that Ottoman institutions needed reform and that foreign European support was essential. After his patron fell he worked as a clerk under the foreign minister and was secretary to the Ottoman delegation at the peace talks at Adrianople in 1829.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He became ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1834 and to the United Kingdom in 1836, and minister for foreign affairs in 1837. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> he won British support for the sultan against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali of Egypt</a> and negotiated the Treaty of Balta Liman, a commercial treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. In the Oriental Crisis of 1840 he stayed with the Great Powers and refused a direct settlement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Muhammad Ali later bribed the Porte to remove him as foreign minister, and he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> as ambassador.</p><p>In 1853, out of office, he regained the foreign ministry, helped to draft the Ottoman response to Russian demands and persuaded the officials who favoured war; the empire declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> on 4 October 1853.</p><p>His part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-gulhane">Edict of Gülhane</a> was central. On 11 August 1839 he sent a private memorandum from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to Lord Palmerston arguing that reform was needed and that the protection and security of the people were required.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edict-of-gulhane">Edict of Gülhane</a>, read in the rose garden of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topkapi-palace">Topkapi Palace</a> before Sultan Abdulmecid and foreign diplomats, opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanzimat">Tanzimat</a> era and called for the protection of individual rights and property, while new laws were to stay within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a>. Between 1845 and 1857 Reşid held the office of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a> six times. He set up commercial courts and a commercial code based on French models, founded the Meclis-i Maarif, a committee of education meant to reduce the ulema’s influence, in 1846, and the Academy of Sciences in 1852.</p><p>French and other pressure repeatedly removed him, and he opposed the 1856 Reform Edict, which he saw as foreign interference. He died of a heart attack in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> on 7 January 1858. His protégés Fuad Pasha and Mehmed Emin Âli Pasha carried the reforms forward.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mustafa Reşid Pasha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 1 July 1839 Mahmud II died suddenly and was succeeded by his son Abdul Mejid I. Reformists such as <b>Mustafa Reşid Pasha</b>, who had been ambassador in London and Paris, argued that European-style institutions could restore imperial power.</blockquote><a href="/movement/tanzimat">Tanzimat · Background</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.96&amp;year=1837" aria-label="Istanbul on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5258 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mustafa Reşid Pasha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5708 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5708 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.96&amp;year=1837">What was here in 1837? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
